{
  "dom_id": "step-q276408",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Solve the inequality $4{x}^{2}\\le 12x$ and express the solution set in interval notation.\n\n$x\\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$",
  "worked_solution": "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the terms to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2} & \\le 12x \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-12x & \\le 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y=4{x}^{2}-12x$ lies **below** the $x$-axis or touches this axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-12x & =0 \\\\ 4x(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x=0$ and $x=3,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029391/assets/d81805f304bd45ddd595bd251202226d2b1ced494fe7816d0bc9f17ee8f998a0.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving \n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}-12x\\le 0,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe look for the part of the parabola that is **below** the $x$-axis or touches this axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $0\\le x\\le 3.$\n\nUsing interval notation, we write $x\\in [0,3].$",
